      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="comparing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How the Replacements Compare with the Old Nautilus Line</h2>
<p>
            The T616 and T618 were good value in their day because they combined a 60-inch deck, a 15% incline and a
            long frame and motor warranty at about $1,150 and $1,500. On the day we checked, Bowflex's sale prices put
            the T6 at $999 and the T9 at $1,499, so a new machine with similar or better capacity now costs about what
            the Nautilus did.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck and capacity</h3>
          <p>
            Both Bowflex machines keep the 60-inch length runners need, and the T9 and T16 widen it to 22 inches. The
            T6's 325 lb rating beats the T616's 300 lb, and the T9 matches the T618's 350 lb. If capacity matters, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a>
            guide explains why a stated limit is only part of the picture.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor ratings</h3>
          <p>
            Nautilus quoted continuous horsepower for the T-series. Bowflex's treadmill page quotes none, and our
            reviews note that Bowflex's figures elsewhere are test ratings. The numbers are not directly comparable;
            our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a>
            explains continuous, peak and test ratings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty and subscriptions</h3>
          <p>
            Bowflex lists a lifetime motor warranty on its treadmill page; Schwinn lists 10 years on frame and motor
            and one year on everything else. Both push the JRNY app. The treadmills run without it, but some console
            features and classes need the subscription, so price that in. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains which terms matter most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Are Nautilus Treadmills on Amazon?</h2>
<p>
            The T616 and T618 were sold on Amazon until 2021, and the recall notice lists Amazon among the sellers. We
            would not buy one there now: any listing still offering a new T616 or T618 would be old stock or
            third-party resale of a recalled product, and the T614 has long been unavailable. This page no longer cards
            a Nautilus model for that reason; the Schwinn 810 card takes the T614's place. Bowflex and Schwinn
            treadmills appear on Amazon at times, but the prices above are from Bowflex's and Schwinn's own stores,
            which also handle warranty registration directly. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explains how to check who the seller is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Nautilus still make treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. On 5 November 2026 nautilus.com sold a vibration plate, supplements and apparel, and no treadmills. Nautilus Inc. sold the Nautilus brand in May 2023, renamed itself BowFlex Inc., and its assets were bought by Johnson Health Tech in April 2024. The Nautilus T614, T616 and T618 are sold only second-hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Nautilus treadmills now?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nobody sells them. The Nautilus brand was sold in 2023, separately from the Bowflex business, and trademark filings now name a Nautilus Brands LLC of New Jersey. Johnson Health Tech bought BowFlex Inc., including the Bowflex, Schwinn and JRNY brands, in 2024, but it does not sell Nautilus-branded treadmills.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Was the Nautilus T618 recalled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. In June 2022 the CPSC announced the recall of about 7,300 T616 and T618 treadmills sold from April to November 2021, which can start on their own. The notice now says the firm is no longer in business and no remedy is available, and tells owners to discard the treadmill and not resell it. Check the serial number under the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the Nautilus T618 specs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sources agree on a 20 by 60-inch running surface, 0 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 350 lb user limit and a folding frame with 26 programs. They disagree on the motor (3.5 CHP in most reviews, 2.75 HP in one table) and the dimensions. It was sold at about $1,300 to $1,500.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Nautilus T616 and T618?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have a 20 by 60-inch deck and 12 mph. The T618 had a stronger motor (3.5 CHP in most sources against 3.0 CHP), a 350 lb rather than 300 lb user rating, 15% incline and a longer warranty. Both were covered by the 2022 recall for units sold from April to November 2021.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What replaced the Nautilus treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No treadmill was sold as a direct replacement, but the closest current machines are from Bowflex and Schwinn, both now owned by Johnson Health Tech. The Bowflex T6 has the T616’s 20 by 60-inch deck with a 325 lb rating, and the T9 matches the T618’s 350 lb with a 22-inch-wide deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I still get parts for a Nautilus treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, from third-party parts suppliers and appliance-parts sites, but the manufacturer is gone and one parts supplier lists Nautilus treadmills as discontinued and no longer supported. Common wear parts are easier to find than console boards. Never repair a recalled T616 or T618 as a way round the recall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Nautilus Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A used Nautilus can be cheap for good reason. These are the mistakes that turn a bargain into a problem.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking a T616 or T618 serial number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">About 7,300 units sold from April to November 2021 can start on their own, and the recall now has no remedy. The serial is on the base frame under the belt. If it falls in the ranges on this page, or the seller will not let you see it, do not buy the treadmill.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the warranty to transfer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Nautilus warranties were for the original buyer, and the company that issued them no longer exists. Johnson Health Tech bought the Bowflex business, not the Nautilus brand. Treat every used Nautilus as sold as-is.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying close to new-machine money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A new Bowflex T6 was $999 on sale on the day we checked, with a full warranty. A ten-year-old T616 with no support is worth a fraction of that. Price a used Nautilus as a walking machine you may have to scrap.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing Nautilus home and commercial products</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Nautilus strength machines in gyms are made under licence by Core Health & Fitness and have nothing to do with the home T-series treadmills. Support or parts for one will not help with the other.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

